# A test that stands in front of a loop sends the WHOLE loop to its other copy where it fails, and with a guess in front that copy is closed. So a guess stands there only where every turn that comes round
# passes the operation that leans on it. `holder` is undefined in the first call and no turn of it reads `holder.step`: asked in front, the test failed and all nine turns ran closed. In a great loop
# (the one that walks up the parents in TypeScript's resolveNameHelper()) some such test fails at most entries.
# f2 = inOneArm, f3 = behindContinue, f4 = inEveryTurn
# OPTIONS: check=Validate
# RUN: walk f2 f3 f4 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# CHECK-NOT: frequency=Rare isLoopHead=true
# CHECK-LABEL: code f3:Body
# CHECK-NOT: frequency=Rare isLoopHead=true
# CHECK-LABEL: code f4:Body
# CHECK: ShapeTest
# CHECK: frequency=Rare isLoopHead=true
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = print;
